.TH PAM_NAMTOR 8 "2026-09-16" "namtor 0.1.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
pam_namtor \- PAM module for Nam-tor infrared face authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth sufficient pam_namtor.so
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_namtor.so
is the PAM authentication module of Nam-tor. It carries no machine-learning
or image-handling code: it resolves the target user, connects to
.BR namtor\-daemon (8)
on
.IR /run/namtor/auth.sock ,
drives one authentication exchange and maps the daemon's verdict to a PAM
return code. Detection, liveness checking and template matching all happen
inside the daemon; the module sees only a pass/fail verdict.
.PP
The module is
.IR fail\-closed .
It returns
.B PAM_SUCCESS
only when the daemon returned a positive match. Every other outcome \(em an
unreachable or unresponsive daemon, a protocol error, a timeout, an unknown
user, an internal panic \(em returns
.B PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
so that the stack falls through to the next module. A fault in Nam-tor can
therefore never grant access, and with the recommended
.B sufficient
control it never denies it either: password authentication proceeds as if
the module were not installed.
.SS Per-service opt-in
Installing the package does
.B not
enable face authentication anywhere. The module is shipped to
.I /usr/lib/security/pam_namtor.so
and no PAM service file is touched, so a fresh install changes no login
path. An administrator opts in one service at a time, either through the
Settings app (which drives the
.I org.namtor.Manager2
system-bus interface) or with
.IR /usr/share/namtor/pam\-install.sh ,
which defaults to
.IR /etc/pam.d/gdm\-password .
.PP
Both paths use the same transactional editor. It inserts a marked block
.RB ( "# namtor\-begin" " \&...\& " "# namtor\-end" )
containing a single
.B auth sufficient pam_namtor.so
line, never deletes or reorders stock lines, refuses the edit unless a
password fallback remains below the inserted block, and keeps a checksummed
backup at
.IR <file>.namtor.bak
for rollback. Removal deletes only the marked block.
.SS Remote authentication
If
.B PAM_RHOST
is set to a non-empty value the module refuses immediately with
.BR PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL ,
before contacting the daemon and regardless of configuration. A face
presented to a camera attached to one machine says nothing about a session
originating on another, so remote logins always fall through to another
authentication factor.
.SS Keypress fallback
While the daemon is working, the module polls standard input. Any keypress
abandons the face attempt and returns
.BR PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL ,
so a user who would rather type a password does not have to wait out the
timeout. The abandoned attempt is timed out by the daemon on its side.
Polling requires a terminal on standard input; in graphical services where
there is none, the attempt simply runs to completion.
.SH OPTIONS
The module recognises no module arguments. Any arguments in the PAM
configuration line are ignored.
.SH MODULE TYPES PROVIDED
Only the
.B auth
module type is implemented.
.B pam_sm_setcred
and
.B pam_sm_acct_mgmt
are present because the PAM ABI requires them and both return
.BR PAM_SUCCESS ;
the module manages no credentials and performs no authorisation.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B PAM_SUCCESS
The daemon matched the presented face against an enrolled template.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
The daemon completed the exchange and reported no match.
.TP
.B PAM_MAXTRIES
The daemon is rate-limiting authentication attempts for this user.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
Face authentication could not be attempted or could not be completed:
the socket is absent, the daemon is unreachable or returned an error,
.B PAM_RHOST
was set, the user is unknown, or a keypress cancelled the attempt. The
stack should fall through to another factor.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/security/pam_namtor.so
The module itself.
.TP
.I /run/namtor/auth.sock
Daemon authentication socket, group
.IR namtor\-auth .
The service invoking the module must be able to reach it.
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/gdm\-password
Default target service for
.IR pam\-install.sh .
Any PAM service file may be used instead.
.TP
.I /usr/share/namtor/pam\-install.sh
Enable the module for one PAM service.
.TP
.I /usr/share/namtor/pam\-uninstall.sh
Remove the module's block from one PAM service.
.TP
.I /usr/share/namtor/pam\-preflight.sh
Check the conditions for a safe edit without changing anything.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NAMTOR_SOCKET
Path to the daemon authentication socket, overriding
.IR /run/namtor/auth.sock .
Intended for testing.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enable face authentication for the GNOME display manager, keeping password
authentication as the fallback:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
namtor\-cli enroll
/usr/share/namtor/pam\-install.sh \-\-target /etc/pam.d/gdm\-password
.fi
.RE
.PP
Disable it again:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
/usr/share/namtor/pam\-uninstall.sh \-\-target /etc/pam.d/gdm\-password
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Use the
.B sufficient
control, and keep a password-capable
.B auth
line below the Nam-tor block; the installer refuses an edit that would not.
Face authentication is a convenience factor: it is not resistant to a
determined attacker with a high-quality artefact, and it should not be the
only way into a machine that holds secrets worth that effort.
.PP
The module logs one line per failure to standard error, which PAM forwards
to the journal. That line carries a fixed reason code and the PAM result
only \(em never embeddings, scores, frames or any other biometric detail.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR namtor\-cli (1),
.BR namtor\-daemon (8),
.BR pam.conf (5),
.BR pam.d (5),
.BR pam (8)
